body:
## Summary

This RFC proposes adopting an OpenAPI-to-TypeScript code generation tool to automatically generate type-safe API clients from our OpenAPI specifications. This will ensure type safety between frontend and backend, reduce boilerplate, and improve developer experience.

## Motivation

Currently, API interactions are manually written, leading to:

- **Type mismatches**: Frontend types can drift from actual backend responses
- **Boilerplate code**: Each endpoint requires repetitive fetch/axios calls, error handling, and type definitions
- **Inconsistent patterns**: Different developers implement API calls differently
- **Manual maintenance**: When APIs change, we must manually update types and API calls

### Benefits of Code Generation

1. **Single source of truth**: OpenAPI spec drives both backend and frontend
2. **Automatic type safety**: Types are always in sync with the API
3. **Reduced boilerplate**: Hooks, types, and API functions generated automatically
4. **Consistency**: All API calls follow the same pattern
5. **Faster development**: No need to write API layer manually
6. **Better DX**: IDE autocomplete for all endpoints, parameters, and responses

---

## Tool Comparison

We evaluated several OpenAPI to TypeScript generators:

| Feature | [Orval](https://orval.dev/) | [openapi-react-query](https://openapi-ts.dev/openapi-react-query/) | [Harness OATS](https://github.com/harness/oats) | [oazapfts](https://github.com/oazapfts/oazapfts) | [openapi-typescript-codegen](https://github.com/ferdikoomen/openapi-typescript-codegen) |
|---------|-------|---------------------|--------------|----------|---------------------------|
| **React Query Hooks** | ✅ Native | ✅ Via wrapper | ✅ Via plugin | ❌ | ❌ |
| **HTTP Client** | Axios/Fetch | Fetch (openapi-fetch) | Custom fetcher | Fetch | Various |
| **Output Modes** | single, split, tags, tags-split | Types + thin wrapper | Configurable | Single file | Multiple files |
| **Customization** | Extensive | Moderate | Moderate | Limited | Moderate |
| **Maintenance** | ✅ Very active | ✅ Active | ⚠️ Moderate | ✅ Active | ⚠️ Declining |
| **Bundle Size** | ~2KB + Axios | ~1KB + fetch | Varies | Minimal | Moderate |

### Option 1: Orval (Recommended)

[Orval](https://orval.dev/) generates complete React Query hooks directly from OpenAPI specs.

**Pros:**
- Native React Query integration with `useQuery`, `useMutation`, `useInvalidate`
- Highly configurable (mutators, hooks, output modes)
- Listed as official TanStack Query community resource
- Very active maintenance (7.18.0 as of writing)

**Cons:**
- Little higher bundle size (2KB)
- Default is Axios, so Axios will be required (we already use axios)

### Option 2: openapi-react-query + openapi-typescript

[openapi-react-query](https://openapi-ts.dev/openapi-react-query/) is a thin (~1KB) wrapper around TanStack Query that works with [openapi-fetch](https://openapi-ts.dev/openapi-fetch/).

**Pros:**
- Minimal bundle size (~1KB wrapper + ~2KB fetch client)
- Uses native Fetch API
- Part of the openapi-typescript ecosystem
- Type inference without code generation for queries

**Cons:**
- Requires more setup (openapi-typescript + openapi-fetch + openapi-react-query)
- Fewer ready-to-use features than Orval

### Option 3: Harness OATS

[Harness OATS](https://github.com/harness/oats) with `@harnessio/oats-plugin-react-query-harness` generates React Query hooks. Tries to emulate openapi-react-query

**Pros:**
- React Query hooks via plugin
- Custom fetcher support
- Operation ID filtering

**Cons:**
- Moderate maintenance activity (last publish ~1 year ago)
- Harness-specific patterns
- Smaller community

---

## Implementation Details

### Final Output Structure

```
src/api/generated/
├── mutator/
│   └── custom-instance.ts         # Custom axios wrapper
└── services/
    ├── sigNoz.schemas.ts          # All shared TypeScript types
    ├── authdomains/
    │   └── index.ts               # Auth domain hooks
    ├── dashboard/
    │   └── index.ts               # Dashboard hooks
    ├── features/
    │   └── index.ts               # Feature flags hooks
    ├── global/
    │   └── index.ts               # Global config hooks
    ├── logs/
    │   └── index.ts               # Logs hooks
    ├── metrics/
    │   └── index.ts               # Metrics hooks
    ├── orgs/
    │   └── index.ts               # Organization hooks
    ├── preferences/
    │   └── index.ts               # User/org preferences hooks
    ├── sessions/
    │   └── index.ts               # Session/auth hooks
    └── users/
        └── index.ts               # User management hooks
```

### Usage Examples

#### Query Hook (GET request)

```typescript
import { useListUsers, useGetUser } from '@services/users';

// List all users
function UsersList() {
  const { data, isLoading, error } = useListUsers();

  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
  if (error) return <Error message={error.message} />;

  return (
    <ul>
      {data?.data?.map(user => (
        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Get single user with path parameter
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { data, isLoading } = useGetUser({ id: userId });

  return <div>{data?.data?.name}</div>;
}
```

#### Mutation Hook (POST/PUT/DELETE request)

```typescript
import { useCreateInvite, useDeleteUser, invalidateListUsers } from '@services/users';
import { useQueryClient } from 'react-query';

function InviteUser() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();

  const createInvite = useCreateInvite({
    mutation: {
      onSuccess: () => {
        // Invalidate and refetch user list
        invalidateListUsers(queryClient);
      },
    },
  });

  const handleInvite = () => {
    createInvite.mutate({
      data: {
        email: 'user@example.com',
        name: 'New User',
        role: 'VIEWER',
      },
    });
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleInvite} disabled={createInvite.isLoading}>
      {createInvite.isLoading ? 'Inviting...' : 'Invite User'}
    </button>
  );
}
```

#### With Path Parameters and Body

```typescript
import { useUpdateUser } from '@services/users';

function EditUser({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const updateUser = useUpdateUser();

  const handleUpdate = (newName: string) => {
    updateUser.mutate({
      pathParams: { id: userId },
      data: { name: newName },
    });
  };

  return <button onClick={() => handleUpdate('Updated Name')}>Update</button>;
}
```

---

## Recommendation: Orval

Recommend **Orval** for the following reasons:

1. **Best React Query integration**: Generates complete, ready-to-use hooks including query keys, invalidation helpers, and mutation options
2. **Active maintenance**: Frequent updates, responsive maintainers
3. **Flexible output**: Different modes gives us organized folder structure by API domain
4. **Community adoption**: Official TanStack Query community resource

Improvement over https://github.com/SigNoz/signoz/issues/8504#issuecomment-3061153364.

One we released this, we have seen then some users have k8s, cloud, host in resource attributes even this all selected results in a query which exceeds the limit.

We should come with a strategy.

1) Always include - `service.name` and `deployment.environment/env/environment` if present in resource filters.
2) Select only one from the list, if k8s is found then don't move to the next. And from k8s just pick the first found
```
{
  "kubernetes": {
    "priority_order": [
      "k8s.pod.uid",
      "k8s.pod.name",
      "k8s.deployment.name"
    ],
    "selection_rule": "pick_first_available"
  },
  "cloud": {
    "priority_order": [
      "cloud.resource_id",
      "cloud.provider",
      "cloud.region"
    ],
    "selection_rule": "pick_first_available"
  },
  "host": {
    "priority_order": [
      "host.id",
      "host.name"
    ],
    "selection_rule": "pick_first_available"
  },
  "container": {
    "priority_order": [
      "container.id",
      "container.name"
    ],
    "selection_rule": "pick_first_available"
  }
}
```

If the resource filter is still empty after the above. We keep this logic https://github.com/SigNoz/signoz/issues/8504#issuecomment-3061153364 and remove the k8s, cloud, host, container from the logic as it is already added above.
